sec.p:
Data provenance and preprocessing. The FF++ and Celeb-DF face-crop frames were prepared by the authors in a preprocessing step that precedes, and is separate from, the training and evaluation scripts, and are stored as image files with one folder per video. The Celeb-DF set holds about 10 frames per clip (65,281 frames from 6,529 videos) and the FF++ set about 37 frames per video (260,793 frames from 7,000 videos). The FF++ frames were extracted from the c23 (light compression) version of the videos. The other extraction settings (face detector, crop margin and sampling interval) are fixed properties of these frame sets, are identical for all three models, and are not varied or tuned in this study, so absolute results should be read as specific to these frame sets. The DFDC crops are a third-party release, the Kaggle dataset “DFDC (Faces) of the train sample’’ [37], which was produced with a multitask cascaded convolutional network (MTCNN)-based face-detection pipeline that keeps detections with confidence of at least 0.9 and saves each retained face as a Portable Network Graphics file (PNG); we use only its train partition, and the release does not document a crop margin or frame stride. FF++ real videos are identified by the source-video number (e.g. 

    000

) and manipulated videos by manipulation and source–target pair (e.g. 

    Deepfakes_000_003

); this identifier is the unit of the split. Celeb-DF and DFDC are used in full except for the frame caps described in Section 3.4, and no cross-domain frame is used for training, checkpoint selection or thresholding.
